      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Natural Logarithm Function",
      "problem": "Given that $y=-9ln⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-9ln⁡x) \\\\  & =-9\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x) \\\\  & =-9\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
