      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating a Sum Containing a Natural Logarithm Function",
      "problem": "Given that $y=2{x}^{e}-ln⁡x$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{e}-ln⁡x) \\\\  & =2e{x}^{e-1}-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(2e{x}^{e-1}-\\frac{1}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =2e\\cdot {1}^{e-1}-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =2e-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
